<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-03">
  <title>Week 3</title>

  <subsection>
    <title>Chapter 3</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Reading Covers 3.1 through 3.5</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Reading Quiz 4 Covers 3.1 through 3.4</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sections 3.1 and 3.2 are a reminder that there is much, much more
            to physics then what we're doing here. The future is exciting! That
            said, whatever kind of physics you end up using, the intuition of
            mechanics will be crucial
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Section 3.3 provides the Newtonian description of gravity, showing how the
            constant acceleration close to the surface of the Earth follows from a mathematical 
            approximation. We will use the shell theorem next week to solve a fun problem,
            but do note worry about the complicated derivation in Note 3.1: you will learn
            a beautiful derivation of the shell theorem in the spring semester.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Section 3.4 describes <q>phenomenological forces</q>. Ultimately, contact forces
            are all electromagnetic and depend on microscopic details of how two objects are touching.
            Fortunately, for forces like the tension in a string and the <q>normal force</q>, we are often
            able to determine them from macroscopic considerations. This usually looks like applying the
            2nd law to an object and saying something like <q>the normal force is responsible for X,
            therefore<ellipsis/></q>. A lesson here is to never try to guess an equation for 
            these forces: find them from the 2nd law.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                An exception to the above is friction. We can make a decent model of friction by saying that
                the sliding friction force is proportional to the contact (normal force) between the two
                objects, with some proportionality coefficient to be determined. Note that static friction is
                different: the force is determined by an inequality, since the surfaces can <q>resist</q>
                relative sliding up to a certain point. Friction forces can often point in unexpected directions:
                thinking about the relative sliding of the two surfaces can be helpful.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Sections 3.5 through 3.7 will be covered in class through next Monday, and you can choose to either
                read them after the Monday class, or try to get ahead this week. Differential equations can be
                a source of anxiety, but please note that any questions involving a differential equation will either
                be about doing an integral, or using a solution to a differential equation to do kinematics.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Why do differential equations show up in mechanics? Well, the acceleration of a particle is 
                <m>\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2}</m>, and often we don't know the acceleration as a function of time,
                but we know it as a function of position. This gives us an equation relating the position
                <m>x(t)</m> to some of its derivatives. What should you know about differential equations?
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                What should you know about differential equations? Two things: First, differential equations of the form 
                <m>\dfrac{dx}{dt}=f(x)</m> can be solved by integration, and we will do an example in class. 
                Usually, as you'll see in the examples, the interesting part is obtaining the differential equation 
                by applying a physical principle
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Second: the solution to the differential equation is a function <m>x(t)</m>, and if we know
                <m>x(t)</m> we can do kinematics. If we need to solve a differential equation that is currently
                out of our mathematical reach but is physically important, like in section 3.7, I will give you
                the solution. The point here is that sometimes the exact form of <m>x(t)</m> is important to
                understand a problem better, but you should not put your focus on the fact that you don't
                know how to solve the differential equation. You will learn how eventually.
            </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>